{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["The sin of Moses and Aaron at the waters of Meribah is not explicitly stated in the verse. Rashi explained (Rashi on Numbers 20:12) that it was because He commanded them, <b>\"and you shall speak to the rock\"</b>, and did not say <b>\"and you shall strike\"</b> — for had they spoken, the Holy One, blessed be He, would have been sanctified before the eyes of all the congregation, and they would say: If this rock, which does not hear and does not speak, fulfills the word of the Holy One, blessed be He, how much more so should we. These are words of Aggadah, but they are not clear, for since He commanded <b>\"Take the staff\"</b>, it is implied that he should strike with it; and if His intention was for speech alone, what is this staff in his hand? So too in the plagues of Egypt, He said <b>\"and the staff that turned into a serpent, take in your hand\"</b> (Exodus 7:15), and that was for striking with it; and sometimes He will say <b>\"Stretch out your hand\"</b> and His meaning is to strike with the staff, for Scripture abbreviates what is already understood. And the miracle of speech is no greater than that of striking, for everything is the same with the rock. Furthermore, why did He say regarding this, <b>\"you broke faith with Me\"</b> (Deuteronomy 32:51)? And the command to speak to the rock — what is recalled in the account of the deed is that He commanded them to speak and it heard, for HaShem would bring water out of this rock, in the manner of <b>\"for it has heard all the words of YHWH\"</b> (Joshua 24:27). And so they did, as it says: <b>\"And Moses and Aaron assembled the congregation before the rock and said to them\"</b> etc. (Numbers 20:10), and behold the rock heard when he said thus to them all.\n\nThere are many arguments among the commentators regarding this sin. Ibn Ezra already refuted many of them. And the secret (sod) that he hinted at is also incorrect, for if Moses lost his intention because of the contention of the people and did not speak to the rock, and therefore water did not come forth the first time, and he went back and struck it a second time with intention cleaving to the whole, and the water came out — behold, they sinned the first time, but it is not fitting to say of this <b>\"you did not believe in Me to sanctify Me\"</b> (Numbers 20:12), for there is no lack of faith at all here.\n\nThe Rav, Rabbi Moshe [Maimonides] (end of Chapter 4 of the Eight Chapters), reasoned about it and said that the sin of Moses our teacher, peace be upon him, was that he inclined toward anger in saying <b>\"Hear now, you rebels\"</b>. HaShem, blessed be He, scrutinized him, that a man like him should become angry before the congregation of the children of Israel where anger is not fitting for him, and anything of this sort in the judgment of that man is a profanation of HaShem's name, because all his motions and all his words were used as instruction, and they would anticipate attaining through them the successes of this world and the world to come. And how could anger be seen upon him when it is among the evil qualities, which does not arise except from an evil disposition of the soul's dispositions? But the statement about him, <b>\"you rebelled against My word\"</b> (Numbers 27:14), is as I shall explain: that he would not speak with fools nor with one who has no standing, but the lowliest woman among them was like Ezekiel son of Buzi, as the Sages recalled (Mekhilta de-Shirah 3), and whatever he would say or do they would scrutinize. And when they saw that he became angry, they would say — if there is no deficiency of quality in him, peace be upon him — were it not that he knew that HaShem was angry with us over the demand for water and that we had angered Him, blessed be He, he would not have been angry. And we did not find that HaShem, exalted be He, was angry in His word to him regarding this matter, but He said: <b>\"Take the staff and assemble the congregation, you and Aaron your brother, and speak to the rock before their eyes, and it shall give its water, and you shall bring forth for them water from the rock, and you shall give drink to the congregation and to their cattle\"</b> (Numbers 20:8).\n\nAnd behold, we have resolved one of the doubts of the Torah, about which many things have been said and which has been asked many times — what sin did he sin? — and see what is said about it and what we have said about it, and the truth will point to its path. These are his words, may his memory be for a blessing.\n\nAnd he added vanity upon vanities: for Scripture says <b>\"you rebelled against My word\"</b> — that they transgressed His word, and said <b>\"you did not believe in Me\"</b> — that they did not believe in Him; the punishment is not because he became angry. And it would have been more fitting for there to be punishment upon Moses when he became angry at the officers of the army (Numbers 31:14) for nothing. And Scripture did not report at all that he became angry, for <b>\"Hear now, you rebels\"</b> is a reproof, in the manner of <b>\"you have been rebels against YHWH\"</b> (Deuteronomy 9:24). Furthermore, Aaron never became angry all his days, for in peace and uprightness he walked from of old. Furthermore, it is impossible that there was not great anger from HaShem against them when they made contention with Moses, and in all the trials in the wilderness their great sin was when they said <b>\"Why did you bring us up from Egypt\"</b> — that they wished to be slaves to those who hate them in crushing labor rather than to be the people of El, like a son who serves his father. And so Scripture said: <b>\"Because you have rejected YHWH who is among you and you wept before Him, saying, Why did we come out of Egypt\"</b> (Numbers 11:20). And the first time they said less than this: <b>\"Why is this, that you brought us up from Egypt to kill me and my children and my cattle with thirst\"</b> (Exodus 17:3), and there was great wrath upon them and much guilt, as it says: <b>\"And he called the name of the place Massah and Meribah, because of the contention of the children of Israel\"</b> etc. And here it is written explicitly: <b>\"These are the waters of Meribah, where the children of Israel contended with YHWH\"</b> (Numbers 20:13). And what sin is greater than this? Woe to one who contends with his Maker! And Moses said: <b>\"Also with me HaShem was angry because of you, saying\"</b> (Deuteronomy 1:37) — if so, they sinned and caused all this evil. Yet according to the Rav's words, they have no sin or transgression in all this matter at all.\n\nAnd as for what he said, <b>\"we did not find in HaShem, blessed be He, that He was angry, but He said, 'Take the staff' etc.\"</b> — know that when they need a thing for their sustenance, even though they complain and sin in it, He is merciful, atones for transgression, and does not arouse all His wrath, and does not mention it, and grants them their request. So too with the first waters He said gently, <b>\"Pass before the people\"</b> etc. (Exodus 17:5), even though there was Massah and Meribah there, which He warned against for all generations (Deuteronomy 6:16). And so with the manna, <b>\"Behold, I will rain down bread for you from heaven\"</b> (Exodus 16:4), in a manner of love and affection — except that He said at the end in the second utterance, <b>\"I have heard your complaints\"</b> (ibid. v. 12), merely informing them of their sin. But when they complain without cause, He pours out His wrath upon them. And here too there is a hint of great wrath and the imposition of plague, as it says: <b>\"And the glory of YHWH appeared to them\"</b> (Numbers 20:6), which hints at the congregation mentioned, and it shows the hand of YHWH that brings plagues, as you will see with the spies (Numbers 14:10) and on the day of Korah (Numbers 16:19) and on the morrow (Numbers 17:7).\n\nAnd wonder at the Rav, for the verse is explicit: <b>\"And they provoked Him at the waters of Meribah, and it went badly with Moses on their account\"</b> (Psalms 106:32), and Scripture counts this sin among the great trials with which they tested YHWH in the wilderness.\n\nThe closest of the things said about this, and the best for rejecting the inquirer, are the words of our Rabbi Hananel, who wrote that the sin was their saying: <b>\"Shall we bring forth for you water from this rock?\"</b> (Numbers 20:10), for it was fitting that they say <b>\"HaShem will bring forth for you water\"</b>, in the manner that they said: <b>\"when YHWH gives you in the evening meat to eat\"</b> etc. (Exodus 16:8), and so in all miracles they should make known that YHWH will do wonders for them. And perhaps the people thought that Moses and Aaron by their wisdom brought forth water for them from this rock, and this is <b>\"you did not sanctify Me\"</b> (Deuteronomy 32:51). And in the first event at the rock He said: <b>\"Behold, I stand before you there, on the rock at Horeb\"</b> (Exodus 17:6), and the seventy elders saw the pillar of cloud standing on the rock, and the miracle was publicized that it was the great deed of HaShem. But here they saw nothing and were misled by the statement of Moses and Aaron. And it is possible that He says of this <b>\"you broke faith with Me\"</b> (Deuteronomy 32:51), for one who derives benefit from consecrated property is called מעילה (misuse). And so <b>\"you rebelled against My word\"</b> (Numbers 27:14), for He commanded <b>\"and you shall speak to the rock before their eyes\"</b> — and the reason is <b>\"that I be sanctified before their eyes\"</b> — or rather you altered My word, from <b>\"and you changed My ordinance\"</b> (Ezekiel 5:6), for I did not command you to speak thus. And <b>\"you did not believe in Me\"</b> (Numbers 20:12) would go out to the children of Israel, or it is an expression of strengthening — you did not strengthen yourselves to sanctify Me before their eyes — from <b>\"and the appointment over the singers\"</b> (Nehemiah 11:23), <b>\"the peg fastened in a secure place\"</b> (Isaiah 22:25).\n\nAnd the truth is that the matter is a great secret among the hidden things of the Torah. For the first time He said to him: <b>\"Behold, I stand before you there, on the rock at Horeb, and you shall strike the rock\"</b> (Exodus 17:6) — He means that His great name was upon the rock at Horeb, which is the glory of YHWH, the consuming fire at the top of the mountain, and therefore he struck only once and abundant waters came out. But here He did not explain this to him, and they both agreed to strike the rock twice, and behold this is the sin. And therefore He said <b>\"you did not believe in Me\"</b> — to place faith in His name, and through faith the miracle would be performed; and it is said <b>\"you rebelled against My word\"</b>, for they rebelled against the spirit of His holiness that is called everywhere <b>\"the mouth of YHWH\"</b>; and therefore He said <b>\"you broke faith with Me\"</b>, and מעילה is nothing but misappropriation. And behold, the sin is mentioned explicitly in the verse. And so the psalmist said: <b>\"before the El of Jacob, who turns the rock into a pool of water\"</b> (Psalms 114:7–8). And so you can understand this in Moses's prayer, where he said: <b>\"YHWH, Elohim, You have begun\"</b> (Deuteronomy 3:24) — for he pleaded before the glorious Name that He forgive him.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of our Sages (Sifrei) who recall anger to him, it is possible that he struck the rock and drops came out in a trickle because of the diminution of his intention due to the anger, and they were astonished at the thing, and they both agreed to strike a second time upon the rock as I have mentioned, and this is the sin upon both of them.\n\nAnd in my opinion, the meaning of <b>\"and you shall speak to the rock\"</b> is like <b>\"upon the rock\"</b>, as in <b>\"Thus says YHWH of hosts concerning the pillars and concerning the sea and concerning the bases: they shall be brought to Babylon\"</b> (Jeremiah 27:19, 22) — He commands that they say before the congregation, while they are all assembled, that HaShem will bring forth water for them from the rock, and so he did. And let it not be difficult for you that it says <b>\"and you shall speak to the rock before their eyes\"</b>, for its meaning is like <b>\"before them\"</b> — that all should hear; as in <b>\"And Hananiah spoke before all the people, saying, Thus says YHWH: So I will break the yoke of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon\"</b> etc. (Jeremiah 28:11), and many similar examples. Or the meaning of <b>\"before their eyes\"</b> here is that the speech would take place while they were assembled there and the rock was before their eyes, as it says in the account of the deed: <b>\"And Moses and Aaron assembled the congregation before the rock\"</b> (Numbers 20:10) — for when they assembled there and saw the rock face to face, they said <b>\"Shall we bring forth for you water from this rock?\"</b>, as the Sages mentioned (Mekhilta Beshalach 6) that they should not say there were springs there. And it is possible that it is to be read as follows: <b>\"Assemble the congregation to the rock, and speak before their eyes, and it shall give its water\"</b>." "And the meaning of <b>\"and it shall give its water\"</b> — that immediately abundant waters would gush forth from it, as it says: <b>\"and abundant water came out\"</b> (Numbers 20:11). For the expression of giving (נתינה) is used for abundance, as in <b>\"and the land shall give its produce, and the tree of the field shall give its fruit\"</b> (Leviticus 26:4), <b>\"for the seed of peace: the vine shall give its fruit, and the land shall give its produce, and the heavens shall give their dew\"</b> (Zechariah 8:12) — all of them promise abundance. And so it is written: <b>\"and streams shall overflow\"</b> (Psalms 78:20). And the meaning of <b>\"its water\"</b> is not like <b>\"shall give its produce\"</b> and <b>\"shall give its fruit\"</b>, for it is not in the nature of a rock to have water in it; rather, the meaning is the water that would come forth from it — for when HaShem turns the flint into a spring of water and water is in it and comes from it, they are called by its name, <b>\"its water\"</b>. And so <b>\"and He will bless your bread and your waters\"</b> (Exodus 23:25), <b>\"his bread is given, his waters are sure\"</b> (Isaiah 33:16). And He said thus to make known that the water would come from the very body of the rock, not from the ground beneath it as is the way of many springs, but from its midst. And so it is written: <b>\"who turns the rock into a pool of water, the flint into a spring of water\"</b> (Psalms 114:8).\n\nAnd He turned and said, <b>\"and you shall bring forth for them water from the rock\"</b> — that while you are still there, you shall bring forth the water from the rock for them. <b>\"For them\"</b> — that all of them shall see it gushing forth. <b>\"And you shall give drink to the congregation\"</b> — that you shall command them to drink before you, and all this is to publicize the miracle. And in the account of the deed, it did not mention thus, <b>\"and he gave drink to the congregation and to their cattle\"</b>, but rather said: <b>\"and the congregation and their cattle drank\"</b> (Numbers 20:11) — for from the intensity of the thirst and when they saw abundant waters flowing, they immediately fell upon the stream and drank.\n\nAnd according to the opinion of our Sages concerning Miriam's well, they said that this rock is the rock that was at Horeb, and therefore they would interpret <b>\"its water\"</b> as the water that it was its custom to give, for now its spring had stopped up with the death of Miriam. For the intention of our Sages regarding Miriam's well is that from the beginning it was a miraculous well, a source of living water flowing wherever the will was upon it. He brought it up with Ishmael in the wilderness of Be'er-sheba, and it burst forth at Horeb from that rock that was there, and in the other journeys it flowed from the rock in the place where they encamped. And when the righteous woman died, the spring ceased, and now it returned through Moses to be for it an opened source from this rock itself, and that is what He said, <b>\"to the rock\"</b> — the known one.\n\nAnd according to the plain sense, there was a rock near the camp, and He commanded: <b>\"and you shall speak to this rock\"</b> that is before you. It is also possible to interpret <b>\"and you shall speak to the rock\"</b> that is before their eyes — meaning the first one that they would see."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra's secret interpretation, here rejected by Ramban, refers to his comment on Numbers 20:1 where he hints at a mystical explanation involving Moses' loss of intention (כוונה) tied to theurgy. Ramban addresses and dismisses multiple interpretations: Rashi's (speak vs. strike), Ibn Ezra's hinted sod, and Maimonides' (anger as the sin), before offering views attributed to Rabbenu Hananel, the Sages (Sifrei), and finally his own kabbalistic reading involving the divine name upon the rock. In the passage beginning 'And the truth is that the matter is a great secret' (והאמת כי הענין סוד גדול), Ramban shifts to his own kabbalistic exegesis."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:26:20.325680598Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}